const title       = 'Do Ravens Have a Sense of Smell?';
const description = 'Ravens have a functional sense of smell but rely primarily on vision and hearing to find food. This page examines raven olfaction, how it compares to other birds, and what the evidence says about whether ravens use smell for locating food or other purposes.';
const path        = '/questions/do-ravens-have-a-sense-of-smell/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',      url: '/' },
  { name: 'Questions', url: '/questions/' },
  { name: 'Do Ravens Have a Sense of Smell?', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Can ravens smell food?', a: 'Ravens can likely smell, but there is little evidence they use olfaction as a primary food-finding strategy. Their food-finding behaviour is dominated by vision (scanning from height, watching for other animals\' reactions, following other ravens) and social information-gathering. Unlike vultures such as the Turkey Vulture — which are well-documented to locate carrion by smell — corvids appear to rely primarily on visual cues. Some carrion-finding ability via smell is plausible but undemonstrated in controlled studies.' },
  { q: 'Do birds have a good sense of smell?', a: 'Bird olfactory ability varies enormously by species. Tube-nosed seabirds (petrels, albatrosses) and New World vultures have highly developed olfaction. Songbirds and corvids generally have smaller olfactory bulbs relative to brain size and are considered less olfaction-dependent than those groups. However, emerging research suggests many birds have more functional olfaction than previously assumed — including possible use in navigation, mate selection, and kin recognition.' },
  { q: 'How do ravens find food if not by smell?', a: 'Ravens are highly visual foragers. They find food primarily through: (1) active visual scanning from elevated perches or during soaring flight; (2) social cue-following — watching the behaviour of other ravens, wolves, and other animals that indicate a carcass or food source; (3) memory — ravens have excellent spatial memory and revisit productive foraging locations; (4) problem-solving and exploration — actively investigating novel objects in their territory. These visual and social strategies are far more important than olfaction for raven food-finding.' },
];
