    <h1>Do Ravens Have a Sense of Smell?</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens have functional olfactory anatomy, but they are primarily visual foragers. There is no strong experimental evidence that ravens use smell as a primary food-finding strategy. Their food-locating behaviour relies mainly on vision, memory, and social information — following other ravens, wolves, and other animals to carcasses and food sources.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Raven Olfactory Anatomy</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens possess olfactory organs — nares (nostrils) and olfactory bulbs in the brain — but these are proportionately smaller relative to overall brain size than in birds known for strong olfaction (such as New World vultures and tube-nosed seabirds). The olfactory bulb ratio (olfactory bulb size relative to total brain size) in corvids is modest, consistent with limited reliance on smell for navigation and foraging.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>Having functional olfactory anatomy does not mean smell is unimportant to ravens — but current evidence does not support the claim that ravens locate food primarily by smell.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>How Ravens Actually Find Food</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens use a suite of vision-and-cognition strategies to locate food:<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>High-altitude scanning:</strong> soaring ravens scan large areas visually. A raven at 500 m altitude can survey several square kilometres.</li>
      <li><strong>Social information:</strong> ravens follow the gaze and flight directions of other ravens to carcasses — documented experimentally by Heinrich (1989). A raven discovering food may recruit others through calls; others follow.</li>
      <li><strong>Following carnivores:</strong> ravens follow wolves, bears, and foxes, exploiting their kills. Wolf-raven associations are well-documented in Yellowstone and Scandinavia.</li>
      <li><strong>Spatial memory:</strong> ravens remember where they cached food and where previous food sources were located — a cognitive rather than olfactory strategy.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Could Ravens Detect Carrion by Smell?</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> At close range, some olfactory cue detection is biologically plausible — the volatile compounds released by decomposing tissue are chemically detectable. However, controlled studies demonstrating that ravens actually use olfaction to find hidden food (as Turkey Vultures clearly do) have not been published. Ravens can find hidden food in experiments, but this is explained by memory and searching behaviour rather than olfaction.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

